 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$\n\n- **III.** ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$\n\n- **IV.** $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba20353a-359a-579a-81b4-ad6b54d23cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459725} {:answer/id #uuid "abe7fc7d-0cee-5635-859d-bdb1efc11301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe7fc7d-0cee-5635-859d-bdb1efc11301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459726} :answer-field/id #uuid "faa90c59-18fa-592c-835f-dd823e7e23bc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460729} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5030960c-213a-5c03-bcab-5063c0a40070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459727} {:answer/id #uuid "abfb5bb1-e557-518d-b1a9-3fb683a8b8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abfb5bb1-e557-518d-b1a9-3fb683a8b8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459728} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dca7c79-0c87-500a-89ac-e5450ae6d72b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460730} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e451b995-7b83-57a1-b453-0753fb215e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459729} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d7f2ab-f0fe-5d95-b870-6cbb5376a51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e451b995-7b83-57a1-b453-0753fb215e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459729} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2bba020-673d-5398-8c82-9a7282ae4c35" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fa08ecc-691c-5923-94fb-19ce5c958e14" :question/math-academy-id "q-21502" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- **II.** ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6=6{x}^{2}-6,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 6(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=-1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519418f0-46ce-521e-aee8-2766f235966a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459749} {:answer/id #uuid "29ca73ed-4234-585f-857f-7ae978a7567d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29ca73ed-4234-585f-857f-7ae978a7567d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459750} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b545ee-1b2a-5dc1-9905-d03bf303ff57" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c50e701-687b-51f1-8681-7be01069f396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459751} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1799eb-53fb-5412-a04b-864186317d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e1799eb-53fb-5412-a04b-864186317d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459752} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2620135-5dcb-5b48-bf58-acc76258f4be" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac754a7f-3579-5d29-b3ee-0bd77fce0bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459753} {:answer/id #uuid "f81c2cab-8400-55bc-9a00-84d2c23ef49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f81c2cab-8400-55bc-9a00-84d2c23ef49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459754} :answer-field/id #uuid "466537a0-143b-51e1-958c-fad736eaa013" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460743} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "490ccb89-606a-573a-bbc2-b7121b826479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459755} {:answer/id #uuid "7fbef945-532b-532a-b6b5-f5e58c104502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fbef945-532b-532a-b6b5-f5e58c104502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459756} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bb5f918-94a1-5adb-b260-d3ec1040aea7" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4e43127-8c70-53b8-9c7f-f4ea673dfe39" :question/math-academy-id "q-2459" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- **III.** ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- **IV.** $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III and IV are true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308c23ff-1eff-5628-8b09-64ca2aebdb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460065} {:answer/id #uuid "287850b7-a355-59c4-aea1-eb7ffebcbb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "287850b7-a355-59c4-aea1-eb7ffebcbb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460066} :answer-field/id #uuid "f976515e-3b8c-585a-9e7d-392f11b1ec8f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "458f04df-9a80-5ac2-9b8f-e4bd017fbdb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460067} {:answer/id #uuid "407acba4-3722-588f-85dc-9922d329d2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "407acba4-3722-588f-85dc-9922d329d2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460068} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd840a5-96a0-5a61-9948-1d1ce4f95701" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460900} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c1e94e8-4982-5977-b24d-9f30a38f35c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460069} {:answer/id #uuid "5681eee1-761e-5e3d-92d2-5e3908c29a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c1e94e8-4982-5977-b24d-9f30a38f35c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460069} :answer-field/id #uuid "36fc8ab0-84b0-50ac-bcd1-914034b5407e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e7b61b7-5224-5508-b07f-2f649466a990" :question/math-academy-id "q-48352" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5]?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n\n- **II.** $g(x)=|x|$\n\n- **III.** $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ but this point does not lie inside $(1,5).$\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is not defined at $x=1$, so it is not continuous at $x=1$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70ba0d5-ffe9-5edf-b595-63ac3db0396d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460071} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9fe983-e606-51a5-ae4d-5ddc12203b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e70ba0d5-ffe9-5edf-b595-63ac3db0396d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460071} :answer-field/id #uuid "91645fcc-dc69-5bc6-96e3-f7f55913e177" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29930833-1889-5e9c-97d4-f7a0f835508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460073} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4c0f44-56da-5894-8c83-7aa7b66a202b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29930833-1889-5e9c-97d4-f7a0f835508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460073} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a096a66-ecfc-56d6-9f40-3995d5185186" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460903} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e02cac9e-ad9d-55d2-931f-4432ae385bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460075} {:answer/id #uuid "9d068bb6-3884-597d-8c6f-dbadfc010489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d068bb6-3884-597d-8c6f-dbadfc010489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460076} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0efc06-b3a1-5982-a7bb-21bd264ae754" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9f8e923-af43-56e4-8d9a-484b2d6f194a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48499" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n- **I.** $g(x)=|x|$\n- **II.** $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- **III.** $h(x)=2x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6073c8a-c89a-5a9c-8ade-98a160287927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460077} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0c3b69-dec6-5f70-9fcc-a97060b38947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6073c8a-c89a-5a9c-8ade-98a160287927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460077} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf08e57-4cd3-53cd-83c6-c7152976ea8d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460905} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a852d6-21fd-5a29-8a75-ae52495112bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460079} {:answer/id #uuid "41eb15e1-19bc-5288-b489-7f2d16632fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41eb15e1-19bc-5288-b489-7f2d16632fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460080} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bd87fb-811c-57b3-ad45-4500d882b299" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8646b0-e817-5a5b-8321-477ab393d592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460081} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7575c0-edd3-5cec-b9e4-43be43554184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab7575c0-edd3-5cec-b9e4-43be43554184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460082} :answer-field/id #uuid "71cede8f-ed10-5809-bb8d-dba1266829b2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9157fc37-ed6c-586e-91c2-30b330ac595f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48689" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of $f(x)$ has a has a sharp edge (cusp) at $x=2.$  Therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)$ does not exist and hence it can't be equal to zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(x)\\le f(2)$ for $x$ near the point $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=2$ since the derivative does not exist at this point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d15cae2-0577-53f3-a1ec-5f70b87de597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459915} {:answer/id #uuid "40f5887a-d56a-553b-a971-3844a56fa608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d15cae2-0577-53f3-a1ec-5f70b87de597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17